[Music]  good morning  i'm wally gilbert and i'm going to talk  to you about  how i change from being a scientist to  an artist  i had a long career in science  as a professor at harvard and then in  biotechnology and for the last 20 years  i've been doing art  i began as a theoretical physicist of  the very mathematical kind  and i took my doctorate in cambridge in  england  and i was an assistant professor of  physics at harvard when i became  interested in molecular biology and  turned into an experimental laboratory  scientist i first worked on  very important problems in molecular  biology  the general problem of how does dna do  anything  i was on a team with jim watson that  discovered messenger rna  i discovered polyribosomes that make  proteins i discovered how the growing  protein chain was actually elongated  and then i worked on how dna was  controlled i isolated the first  repressor a control protein that turns  the gene off i isolated the dna region  that that protein bound to to control  the gene  and i worked out that first dna sequence  for that particular dna region 20 bases  long it's actually one of the first long  dna sequences that was done  and it took us two years  one base a month  and then in 1976  i discovered a rapid method to work out  dna sequence  one could do hundreds of bases in an  afternoon rather than one base a month  and that made it possible to work out  genes entirely fred sanger in england  worked out another method of similar  power at the same time  the world began to use these two methods  to sequence genes  ten years later by the middle eighties  eight million bases of dna had been  sequenced  my nobel prize in 1980  is the discovery of this fast dna  sequencing and fred sanger and i shared  the nobel prize  the three billion basis of the human  genome was first sequenced by 2000  taking about 10 years  so we had gone from impossible to being  able to completely sequence a human  being  and by now they're machines that can  sequence the human genome in 30 minutes  science is in general the effort to  discover new truths about the world  richard feynman once described the  excitement of discovery  as being that wonderful moment in which  you know something about the world that  no one else yet knows  it's a true true fact about scientific  discovery is very evanescent because the  moment you discover something you then  proceed to tell somebody else about it  as scientists we have the creative drive  to discover or to make something new  that's never been made before  but in science that new thing must be  true  that's a social construct and science is  a socially constructed  effort  the truth we find by experiment  an experiment means a process  that other scientists can repeat and  convince themselves of its  truth  or by mathematical proof that other  scientists agree is correct  and can follow and do the artist also  has the same creative drive to discover  to make something new not made before  but an art  we don't have a criterion of truth we  have a criterion of beauty  the social construct is other people  like the art and will buy it  nowadays i do art i began 20 years ago  when i discovered that i could make a  six foot image using a two megapixel  digital camera  at that time people would tell me oh i  can make a postcard size picture  but you really have to have high  resolution which didn't exist then in  order to make bigger pictures i  discovered this was not true  and you can make very effective large  pictures that carry an emotional impact  when i discovered this i said to myself  this is an art form  and i'm going to show you the sorts of  pictures i make and describe my changes  over the years you must visualize the  images i shall show you  as a three or six foot image as they are  in reality  unfortunately you're going to see them  these on the computer screen  and they're rather small if i were  speaking to you in a lecture hall you  would at least see large images  projected on the wall  as i said i began with a two megapixel  camera  and could make an image this image which  is six foot in size  of a  sailboat this image is actually taking  pictures actually taken in egypt another  picture is i call doors it's actually  taken in a  museum in spain i'm walking through  before any pictures have been hung on  the walls and i see this scene in three  doorways and simply snap a photograph  the picture of columns in berlin  in the first years this is around 2002 i  was taking pictures of fragments of the  world  and then i began to do a series of  photographs just of graffiti  paint that i found on buildings  and these are  graffiti in spain  i traveled in in  italy  and took pictures of marzipan candies  in taramina in sicily  this is a  totally  imitation tangerine it's actually a  candy tangerine everything is made of  sugar  and here is marzipan peaches  in late  around 19 2006  i was invited to come to poland and  photograph  in an old factory  and i did a series of images  paint uh grease on a metal surface  paper from a press  an old firebox  a rivet  these are i call wheels but they're  actually  templates for sand casting  a fragment of a machine  a fragment of bricks on a windowsill  and we went to warsaw  and did a insulation in the factory of  30 two by three foot images  and 26 8 foot by 12 foot hangings  this picture that i show you of bricks  was in fact one of those hangings  and there is pictures from the  exhibition  a very big hanging with a woman staring  at some other another image beneath it  there are people  wandering around staring at these big  hangings of the fragments of the  machines  hung up in a  old  factory setting which contained the  machines  continue photographing looking at little  elements of the world  a bicycle lock  fragment of a chain a fragment of a boat  seen against the sky  i was entranced by patterns  which give you a feeling of the material  and the object but as i said because  these are fragments of the world they  become essentially abstracts here's a  still life  actually in the orkney islands there's a  little scene of nature again from the  orkney islands  the scene of  two cheeses  in a market in poland  again the new york new islands  a picture i call not seaworthy  an ancient boat sort of falling apart  a picture that's actually in turkey  there's simply a still life of a  clothes on the line  in around 2008 i did photographs of  ballet dancers in rehearsal  and on the stage  and those pictures appeared in a book  called behind the scenes at boston  ballet  i found the ballet dancers beautiful to  photograph  and exciting is  when you have something like this a jump  another ballet dancer jumping  a scene from a valley called serenade  another scene from a  ballet russian ballet  a ballet dancer in  waiting and rehearsal  i love these pictures  but  and i said we made a book with a number  of these pictures in it but i couldn't  get permission to use some of the  pictures that i really enjoyed  and so this picture for example i could  not use the original and so i made a  totally abstract outline from it  one dancer throwing the other another in  the air  and so i spent some time playing just  with outlines of dancers  i made these abstract profiles  abstracting the image the photograph in  the computer  and then i took  just this image of a head  and took the outline of the head and  shrank it down over and over again to  produce an image i call vanishing and  the original image here  is about eight feet wide and ten feet  high  printed on canvas  once i made that image and stared at it  looking at the three dimensional quality  of the lines  i decided i was fascinated by the lines  and the textures and began to play with  them  here's an image i call four faces  based on the image i just showed you  and then by taking these black and white  images  and superimposing them and adding colors  in the computer  i could make images 